└── Modbus TCP client for reading holding registers from a power meter
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, umodbus, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, connect, read_holding_registers, isError,
    │   registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus response objects expose .isError() and 
    │   .registers; minimalmodbus raises exceptions on error and returns raw 
    │   register lists directly; minimalmodbus is primarily built for serial 
    │   (RTU) communication; TCP support is limited/less idiomatic; pyModbusTCP
    │   read_holding_registers returns None on failure instead of an object 
    │   with isError(); modbus-tk uses a different client/master construction 
    │   pattern (modbus_tcp.TcpMaster) and exception-based error handling; 
    │   Unit/slave id parameter naming and position differs across libraries 
    │   (unit= vs slaveaddress= vs slave_id=); Connection lifecycle methods 
    │   differ: connect()/close() naming and auto-reconnect behavior vary 
    │   between libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All these packages implement Modbus master/client functionality capable
        of reading holding registers over TCP, making them candidates to 
        replace pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient. pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk are 
        TCP-focused Modbus master libraries with comparable read-register APIs.
        minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial/RTU communication and 
        has a different response model (raises exceptions, returns raw lists 
        instead of a response object with .isError()/.registers), so while 
        functionally it can read holding registers, adapting it requires 
        non-trivial glue code as noted in the file's own docstring. umodbus 
        supports both client and server roles and could serve as a client 
        replacement with API adaptation.

