    │   self.state attribute
    ├── Pitfalls   : automat requires converting the class to use 
    │   @MethodicalMachine.state()/@MethodicalMachine.input()/@MethodicalMachin
    │   e.output() decorators instead of imperative add_transition calls; 
    │   python-statemachine requires declaring States as class attributes and 
    │   transitions via `State.to()` syntax, not runtime add_transition calls 
    │   on an instantiated Machine; state_machine (pluggableenterprise) has a 
    │   similarly different declarative class-based API; transitions 
    │   dynamically injects trigger methods (self.start_fill(), self.trip(), 
    │   etc.) onto the model instance -- none of the alternatives replicate 
    │   this exact dynamic-attachment behavior; self.state access/mutation 
    │   semantics differ across libraries and may need explicit property 
    │   wiring; conditions= and after= callback wiring syntax differs across 
    │   all alternatives
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages provide declarative finite-state-machine engines with
        transition/guard/callback semantics that could conceptually replace 
        `transitions` for managing IDLE/FILLING/HOLDING/DRAINING/FAULT states. 
        However, `transitions` uses a dynamic mixin/model pattern (Machine 
        bound to `self`, generating trigger methods at runtime and exposing 
        `self.state`), whereas automat uses decorator-based MethodicalMachine 
        definitions, and python-statemachine/state_machine use 
        class-attribute-based State/Transition declarations. None are drop-in 
        API matches.

