  ⚠ Remaining incompatibilities noted by connector LLM:
    - No runtime Machine(model=self, states=..., initial=...) constructor 
equivalent exists in python-statemachine; it requires declarative class-level 
State/transition definitions instead of imperative construction bound to an 
external model instance.
    - transitions' add_transition() API (with conditions=, after=, wildcard '*'
source, and list-of-sources support) has no direct runtime equivalent in 
python-statemachine, which wires guards/callbacks/multiple-source transitions 
declaratively via State.to() at class definition time.
    - transitions dynamically injects trigger methods (start_fill, 
level_reached, start_drain, empty, trip) onto the model instance (self) at 
runtime; python-statemachine attaches transitions as descriptors on its own 
StateMachine class, not onto an arbitrary externally-supplied model object -- 
this dynamic-attachment behavior cannot be replicated with a wrapper function.
    - self.state read/write semantics (used directly in step()) differ between 
the two libraries and require explicit property/state-model wiring that changes
the class's structure, not just call-site glue.
