Equivalence Groups Found
└── finite-state machine engine for modeling stateful process control (states, 
    transitions, guards/conditions, transition callbacks)
    ├── Candidates : transitions, python-statemachine, automat
    ├── Used APIs  : Machine(model=self, states=STATES, initial=...), 
    │   add_transition(trigger, source, dest, conditions=..., after=...), 
    │   wildcard source '*' for global transitions, self.state (implicit model 
    │   attribute set by Machine), dynamically injected trigger methods 
    │   (start_fill, level_reached, start_drain, empty, trip)
    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions dynamically injects trigger methods (e.g. 
    │   start_fill) onto the model object at runtime; automat uses a 
    │   MethodicalMachine descriptor/decorator pattern (@machine.state(), 
    │   @machine.input(), @machine.output()) requiring the class to be 
    │   rewritten around decorators rather than post-hoc Machine() wiring; 
    │   python-statemachine defines states/transitions as class-level State() 
    │   and .to()/.from_() declarations on a StateMachine subclass, 
    │   incompatible with the current 'attach Machine to arbitrary model 
    │   instance' pattern; wildcard '*' source transitions (used for 'trip') 
    │   are supported natively in transitions but require manual enumeration of
    │   all states in automat/python-statemachine; conditions='can_fill' 
    │   string-based method lookup in transitions differs from automat's 
    │   explicit input/output wiring and python-statemachine's guard/validators
    │   callables; self.state attribute name and semantics differ across 
    │   libraries and must be re-mapped; docstring itself calls out that this 
    │   is a Category C substitution requiring the FSM to be re-encoded into a 
    │   structurally different machine, not a drop-in replacement
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries provide declarative/programmatic 
        finite-state-machine construction with named states, guarded 
        transitions, and callback hooks, which is the exact pattern used here 
        (Machine + add_transition + conditions/after callbacks). They are 
        genuine functional alternatives for implementing the same 
        TankController FSM, though each has a very different API surface and 
        object model.

