object' constructor. smtplib.SMTP(host, po
  ⚠ Remaining incompatibilities noted by connector LLM:
    - smtplib's connection-oriented object model (a stateful SMTP session with 
.sendmail(sender, recipients, raw_message)) has no structural counterpart in 
apprise, which is a stateless, service-URL/config-driven notify() call — 
bridging this requires reconstructing message semantics (parsing 
From/To/Subject back out of a raw string) rather than a thin adapter, and the 
two libraries' data models are fundamentally different rather than just 
differently-named.
    - The EmailNotifier class's dependency-injection pattern (`smtp=None` 
defaulting to a directly-instantiated SMTP connection object) assumes an 
injectable transport with SMTP protocol semantics (host/port/sendmail); apprise
does not support injecting a raw socket/connection in the same way, so tests 
that swap in a fake SMTP object for `_default_smtp()` would need an entirely 
different mocking strategy built around apprise's Apprise()/notify() surface, 
not merely a wrapper matching one method's signature.
    - Error handling and delivery-confirmation semantics differ irreconcilably 
at the architecture level: smtplib raises specific exceptions 
(SMTPRecipientsRefused, SMTPHeloError, etc.) that calling code may already 
catch, while apprise reports success/failure as a boolean from notify(); a 
wrapper can approximate this by raising a generic exception on False, but 
cannot reproduce the granular smtplib exception hierarchy, meaning any code 
depending on specific smtplib exception types elsewhere would remain broken.

