Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers to industrial field
    devices
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient(host, port=port), client.connect(), 
    │   client.read_holding_registers(address, 1, unit=self.unit), 
    │   rr.isError(), rr.registers[0], client.write_register(address, counts, 
    │   unit=self.unit), wr.isError()
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus's read/write calls return response objects with 
    │   .isError() and .registers; pyModbusTCP typically returns None or raises
    │   on error and returns lists/values directly -- error handling logic in 
    │   _read1/write_valve must be rewritten; modbus-tk uses a 
    │   master.execute(slave_id, cst.READ_HOLDING_REGISTERS, address, count) 
    │   call pattern with exceptions for errors, not a boolean isError() flag 
    │   -- requires substantial glue code; unit/slave id parameter naming and 
    │   position differs across libraries (unit= vs slave_id positional); 
    │   connection lifecycle methods differ (connect()/close() naming and 
    │   auto-reconnect behavior); minimalmodbus was suggested as a candidate 
    │   but is primarily designed for serial/RTU communication, not TCP, and 
    │   has a fundamentally different read/write API and error model as noted 
    │   in the file's own docstring -- rejected as non-viable without heavy 
    │   glue
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries implement Modbus TCP master/client functionality 
        capable of reading and writing holding registers over TCP, which is the
        sole I/O pattern used in this file (ModbusIO._read1 and write_valve). 
        pyModbusTCP offers a similarly shaped ModbusClient with 
        read_holding_registers/write_single_register methods and boolean error 
        signaling, making it a closer drop-in than minimalmodbus (which is 
        serial/RTU-focused and lacks a comparable TCP-oriented, 
        unit-id-parameterized API) or umodbus (which is primarily 
        server/framework-oriented, not a general-purpose master client). 
        modbus-tk also provides Modbus TCP master capability but with a 
        materially different execute()-based call pattern and error handling 
        via exceptions rather than an isError() result object.

