    │   response.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial (RTU) 
    │   communication; TCP support is limited/absent, requiring additional glue
    │   or a different transport approach; minimalmodbus and modbus-tk do not 
    │   return an object with isError(); errors are typically raised as 
    │   exceptions, so error-handling logic must be rewritten; Register 
    │   addressing and unit/slave-id parameter conventions differ across 
    │   libraries (e.g., pymodbus's unit= kwarg vs modbus-tk's slave id in 
    │   constructor); Return types differ: pymodbus returns a response object 
    │   with .registers list, while modbus-tk/minimalmodbus typically return 
    │   raw values or lists directly; Connection lifecycle management 
    │   (connect/close) differs between libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries provide Modbus master/client functionality capable 
        of reading and writing holding registers over TCP (or serial). 
        pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient exposes 
        read_holding_registers/write_register with a response object supporting
        isError(); modbus-tk's TcpMaster provides equivalent read/write execute
        calls; minimalmodbus is primarily RTU/serial-oriented but does expose 
        read_register/write_register semantics and is explicitly called out in 
        the file's own docstring as a substitution target requiring glue code 
        due to a different API/error model.

