    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus response objects expose .isError() and 
    │   .registers[0]; minimalmodbus and modbus-tk return raw values or raise 
    │   exceptions instead, requiring rewritten error-handling logic; 
    │   pymodbus's 'unit' keyword (slave/unit id) maps differently across 
    │   libraries (e.g., minimalmodbus sets slave address at client 
    │   construction, not per-call); pyModbusTCP client API uses ModbusClient 
    │   with methods like read_holding_registers returning a list or None on 
    │   failure, not an object with isError(); minimalmodbus is primarily 
    │   serial-oriented (RS-485/RS-232); TCP support is limited/less mature 
    │   compared to pymodbus; Connection/lifecycle management (connect/close) 
    │   differs across all these libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages implement Modbus master/client functionality capable 
        of reading and writing holding registers over TCP, which is the sole 
        operation performed in this file (ModbusIO wraps a dependency-injected 
        client). pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk both provide dedicated TCP master 
        clients with comparable request/response semantics. minimalmodbus is 
        explicitly called out in the file's own docstring as a substitution 
        target, though it is primarily designed for serial (RTU/ASCII) 
        communication and its TCP support and API (single-value read/write 
        functions returning raw values rather than response objects with 
        .isError()/.registers) differ substantially from pymodbus's 
        object-based protocol design.

