Equivalence Groups Found
└── HTTP client for synchronous REST API calls (session-based GET/POST with 
    JSON payloads)
    ├── Candidates : requests, httpx, aiohttp
    ├── Used APIs  : Session, session.get, session.post, response.json, 
    │   response.raise_for_status, response.status_code
    ├── Pitfalls   : httpx.Client has slightly different exception hierarchy 
    │   (httpx.HTTPStatusError vs requests.HTTPError) and different timeout 
    │   semantics (single float vs Timeout object); httpx does not raise 
    │   ConnectionError the same way; error types need to be mapped; aiohttp 
    │   requires async def methods, 'async with session.get(...)' context 
    │   managers, and an event loop — this is an architectural change, not a 
    │   drop-in replacement; aiohttp's response.json() is a coroutine (must be 
    │   awaited) unlike requests/httpx which return parsed data synchronously; 
    │   Session lifecycle management differs: aiohttp.ClientSession must be 
    │   created/closed within an async context
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file wraps a requests.Session to perform GET/POST calls, parse 
        JSON, and raise on HTTP errors. httpx.Client exposes a nearly identical
        synchronous API (get, post, raise_for_status, json) and can be 
        substituted with mostly import/instantiation changes. aiohttp offers 
        the same conceptual role (HTTP client session) but is async-only, so it
        is a valid functional alternative in principle though it requires 
        converting all call sites to async/await.

