Equivalence Groups Found
└── synchronous HTTP client (session-based, with request methods, 
    status/exception handling, JSON parsing)
    ├── Candidates : requests, httpx, aiohttp
    ├── Used APIs  : Session, session.get, session.post, r.raise_for_status, 
    │   r.json, r.status_code
    ├── Pitfalls   : httpx.Client vs requests.Session: timeout semantics differ
    │   (httpx uses Timeout objects), exception classes differ 
    │   (httpx.HTTPStatusError vs requests.HTTPError), 'json=' kwarg for POST 
    │   works similarly but response streaming/redirect defaults may differ; 
    │   aiohttp requires switching the whole call chain (get_demand, 
    │   get_runtimes, command) to async def and awaiting all I/O calls, plus 
    │   using aiohttp.ClientSession instead of a plain Session object - not a 
    │   drop-in replacement; raise_for_status behavior/exception types differ 
    │   across libraries and calling code that catches requests-specific 
    │   exceptions would break
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file uses requests.Session as an injectable client to perform 
        get/post calls, check status via raise_for_status, and parse JSON 
        responses. httpx.Client exposes a very similar synchronous API (get, 
        post, raise_for_status, json, status_code) and can be swapped in with 
        modest glue code, as the docstring itself notes. aiohttp offers the 
        same conceptual role (HTTP client session) but is fundamentally async 
        (ClientSession, async context managers, await on 
        .get()/.post()/.json()), so while it serves the same functional role, 
        it cannot be substituted without converting ScadaClient's synchronous 
        methods to async/await throughout.

